window.QUIZZES_HAND={"js1":[{"q":"You run a script in Node and get `ReferenceError: document is not defined`. What does that mean?","options":["You forgot to import document from a module","document must be declared with let","document is a browser API and Node is not a browser","Node is installed incorrectly on this machine"],"answer":2,"why":"The language is identical everywhere; document, window and localStorage are supplied by the browser, and process, fs and require by Node.","whyWrong":["There is nothing to import (it does not exist in Node at all.)","It is not a variable you declare.","","A broken install fails differently and much earlier."]},{"q":"Which of these is part of the ECMAScript language rather than a host API?","options":["localStorage","process","Promise","fetch"],"answer":2,"why":"Promise is specified by ECMAScript, so it exists in every JavaScript runtime.","whyWrong":["A browser API.","A Node API.","","Supplied by the host, it was browser-only until Node 18."]},{"q":"Why does `console.log` work in both the browser and Node despite not being part of the language?","options":["It is a reserved word in the ECMAScript grammar","It was standardized as part of ECMAScript in the ES2015 revision","Node ships a polyfill that reimplements the browser's console","Both hosts happen to provide their own implementation of it"],"answer":3,"why":"Two separate hosts each chose to provide an object with that name and similar behavior. It is convention, not specification.","whyWrong":["It is an ordinary identifier.","It has never been part of the language specification.","Node implements its own; it does not borrow the browser's.",""]}],"js2":[{"q":"What does `typeof null` return?","options":["\"null\"","\"undefined\"","It throws","\"object\""],"answer":3,"why":"A bug from 1995 that cannot be fixed without breaking the web. Check with `value === null` instead.","whyWrong":["Reasonable, and not what happens.","That is typeof undefined.","typeof never throws.",""]},{"q":"Why is `0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3` false?","options":["JavaScript rounds all arithmetic to 15 digits","0.3 is stored as a string","Binary floating point cannot represent 0.1 exactly","=== compares references for numbers"],"answer":2,"why":"Every JavaScript number is a 64-bit float, and C, Java and Python agree on this result. Never use floats for money.","whyWrong":["There is no such rounding rule.","It is a number literal.","","Numbers are primitives, compared by value."]},{"q":"Which check correctly detects an array?","options":["Array.isArray(x)","typeof x === \"array\"","x instanceof Object","x.length !== undefined"],"answer":0,"why":"typeof reports \"object\" for arrays, and instanceof breaks across realms such as iframes.","whyWrong":["","typeof never returns \"array\".","True for every object, including plain ones.","A string has length too."]}],"js3":[{"q":"`const user = { name: 'Ada' }; user.name = 'Grace';`, what happens?","options":["It works only in sloppy mode","It works: const freezes the binding, not the object","TypeError: assignment to constant variable","It silently does nothing"],"answer":1,"why":"const prevents the name being pointed at a different value. Object.freeze is what makes the contents immutable.","whyWrong":["Strict mode does not change this.","","That is what reassigning `user` itself would throw.","The mutation really happens."]},{"q":"Why does `for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) setTimeout(() => console.log(i))` print 3, 3, 3?","options":["setTimeout batches identical callbacks","The loop runs after the timeouts","console.log reads the final value of the loop","var is function-scoped, so all three closures share one i"],"answer":3,"why":"let creates a new binding per iteration, which is why swapping it prints 0, 1, 2.","whyWrong":["No such batching exists.","The loop finishes first, which is the cause, but the sharing is the mechanism.","It reads the shared variable, which happens to be 3 by then.",""]},{"q":"Reading a `let` variable before its declaration throws a ReferenceError. What is that region called?","options":["The temporal dead zone","The closure gap","The hoisting window","Strict scope"],"answer":0,"why":"var would give you a silent undefined instead, which lets the bug run on and fail somewhere else.","whyWrong":["","Not a term.","Not a term, hoisting is what puts the declaration there.","Not a term."]}],"js4":[{"q":"What is `1 + '2'`?","options":["A TypeError","NaN","3","\"12\""],"answer":3,"why":"If either side of + is a string it concatenates. Every other arithmetic operator coerces to number instead, which is why 1 - \"2\" is -1.","whyWrong":["JavaScript coerces rather than throwing.","No conversion fails here.","That would require both to be numbers.",""]},{"q":"Which of these is TRUTHY?","options":["0","NaN","\"\"","\"0\""],"answer":3,"why":"Only eight values are falsy: false, 0, -0, 0n, \"\", null, undefined and NaN. A non-empty string is truthy however it looks.","whyWrong":["One of the eight falsy values.","One of the eight.","One of the eight.",""]},{"q":"`config.port || 3000` versus `config.port ?? 3000`, when do they differ?","options":["When port is null","When port is undefined","When port is 0 or an empty string","They never differ"],"answer":2,"why":"|| falls back on any falsy value, so a legitimate port of 0 becomes 3000. ?? falls back only on null and undefined.","whyWrong":["Both fall back on null.","Both fall back on undefined.","","They differ on exactly the falsy-but-not-nullish values."]}],"js5":[{"q":"`let s = 'hello'; s.toUpperCase();`, what is s afterwards?","options":["\"hello\", strings are immutable and the result was discarded","\"\", since toUpperCase clears the receiver before it returns","\"HELLO\", since the method rewrites the variable in place","It throws, because a primitive string has no methods to call"],"answer":0,"why":"Every string method returns a new string. You must assign the result.","whyWrong":["","No string method empties its receiver, or changes it at all.","It returns a new string. Nothing is written back to s.","The primitive is wrapped automatically, so the call is valid."]},{"q":"What does `Number('')` return?","options":["NaN","0","undefined","It throws"],"answer":1,"why":"An empty form field silently becomes zero, which is why a safe parse checks for an empty string BEFORE converting.","whyWrong":["That is Number(\"abc\").","","Number always returns a number.","It does not throw."]},{"q":"Why should money never be stored in a JavaScript `number`?","options":["Floating-point error compounds; use integer cents","Numbers cannot exceed 2^32 without rounding","toFixed rounds unpredictably","Currency symbols cannot be stored alongside"],"answer":0,"why":"0.1 + 0.2 is not 0.3, and the error accumulates across many operations.","whyWrong":["","Integers stay exact up to 2^53.","toFixed rounds predictably; the underlying value is the problem.","Symbols live in formatting; they were never the number's job."]}],"js6":[{"q":"Why must a `switch` include a `default` branch?","options":["A switch without default is a syntax error","Without one, an unexpected value silently returns undefined","Strict mode requires an exhaustive switch","The default branch runs before any case is tested"],"answer":1,"why":"That silent undefined is exactly how a validation function fails open.","whyWrong":["default is optional syntactically.","","Strict mode does not require it.","It runs last, only when nothing matched."]},{"q":"`switch (1)`, does `case \"1\"` match?","options":["Yes: switch coerces like ==","Only for single-character strings","No: switch compares with === and never coerces","Only in sloppy mode"],"answer":2,"why":"So normalize casing or type before switching if you need loose matching.","whyWrong":["switch is strict.","Length is irrelevant.","","Mode does not affect it."]},{"q":"What does `'a' && 'b'` evaluate to?","options":["false","true","\"b\"","\"a\""],"answer":2,"why":"&& and || return one of their operands, not a boolean: && gives the last value when all are truthy, || gives the first truthy one.","whyWrong":["Neither operand is falsy.","They do not coerce their result to a boolean.","","That is what 'a' || 'b' gives."]}],"js7":[{"q":"Iterating an array with `for...in`, what type are the keys?","options":["Whatever the element type is","Strings (so i + 1 gives \"01\", not 1)","Numbers, since indexes are numeric","Symbols, one per index"],"answer":1,"why":"for...in also walks the prototype chain. Never use it on an array, use for...of.","whyWrong":["It yields keys, not values.","","That is the assumption that causes the bug.","Only string keys are enumerated."]},{"q":"Which loop form supports `break` AND iterates values?","options":["for...in","for...of","forEach","map"],"answer":1,"why":"forEach cannot break because you gave control of the loop to the library; for...in gives you keys.","whyWrong":["It yields keys.","","It ignores return values and cannot break.","It builds a new array and cannot break."]},{"q":"You need both the index and the value of each array element. What is the idiomatic form?","options":["for (const v of arr)","for (const [i, v] of arr.entries())","arr.forEach(v => ...)","for (const i in arr)"],"answer":1,"why":"entries() yields [index, value] pairs which destructure directly, and the index is a real number.","whyWrong":["Gives no index at all.","","forEach's second parameter works, but it cannot break.","Gives string keys and walks the prototype chain."]}],"js8":[{"q":"`function welcome(name, greeting = 'Hello')`, when does the default apply?","options":["Whenever the argument is omitted or empty","Only when the argument is undefined","When the argument is falsy","When the argument is null or undefined"],"answer":1,"why":"null, 0 and \"\" are real values, so they are passed through. That is the difference from a hand-rolled || fallback.","whyWrong":["An empty string is a value, not an omission.","","That would be a || fallback.","That would be a ?? fallback."]},{"q":"Why should an arrow function not be used as an object method that needs `this`?","options":["Arrows are always asynchronous","Arrows cannot be object properties","An arrow has no own this; it uses the outer one","this is undefined inside every arrow"],"answer":2,"why":"Use an arrow for callbacks and a regular function for methods.","whyWrong":["They are not.","They can be, they just behave differently.","","It inherits a this, which is usually the wrong one."]},{"q":"What is wrong with `return` on its own line followed by `{ ok: true };`?","options":["The object literal on the next line parses as a block and throws at runtime","It returns a Promise that resolves with the object literal","A semicolon is inserted after return, so the function returns undefined","Nothing is wrong, the parser joins the two lines into one"],"answer":2,"why":"Automatic semicolon insertion is correct nearly everywhere and wrong here. Keep the brace on the return line.","whyWrong":["It does parse as a block, but the function returned before reaching it.","No promise is involved; the function is not async.","","The parser does the opposite: it splits them and returns undefined."]}],"js9":[{"q":"What is the mechanical fix for deeply nested conditionals?","options":["Extract each branch into its own class","Replace the nested ifs with a switch","Guard clauses: handle the exceptional cases first","Combine every condition into one && expression"],"answer":2,"why":"The main path then stays at the left margin and reads top to bottom.","whyWrong":["Overkill, and it moves the nesting rather than removing it.","Only helps when branching on one value.","","That produces one unreadable condition and loses the specific error per case."]},{"q":"What is a pure function?","options":["One that never returns undefined, whatever it is given","One written as an arrow function rather than as a function declaration","One that takes no parameters and reads only constants","Same input gives same output, and it touches nothing outside itself"],"answer":3,"why":"Pure functions need no mocks, no setup and no teardown to test, which is why you concentrate impurity at the edges.","whyWrong":["A pure function may legitimately return undefined.","Syntax has nothing to do with purity.","Parameter count is unrelated; a pure function usually has some.",""]},{"q":"A function is named `getUser` and it also creates one when absent. What is the problem?","options":["It should be async","get functions cannot have side effects in JavaScript","The name lies, and every future reader is misled","It needs more parameters"],"answer":2,"why":"Names are the cheapest documentation available, and a wrong one is worse than none.","whyWrong":["Unrelated.","Nothing in the language forbids it, the problem is human.","","Unrelated."]}],"js10":[{"q":"`const o1 = { n: 1 }; const o2 = o1; o2.n = 99;`, what is `o1.n`?","options":["1, because o2 is a copy of the object","It throws because o1 is const","99 (both names refer to the same object)","undefined, the write did not land"],"answer":2,"why":"Objects are copied by reference. This explains why a function can 'change my data' and why comparing objects with === is rarely what you want.","whyWrong":["Assignment copies the reference, not the object.","const protects the binding, not the contents.","","The property is there, holding 99."]},{"q":"`const copy = { ...user }` then `copy.address.city = 'Bath'`. What happened to `user.address.city`?","options":["It became undefined on the original","It is unchanged, the copy is independent","It throws, the nested object is frozen","It changed too (spread is a shallow copy)"],"answer":3,"why":"Nested objects are still shared references. structuredClone gives you a real deep copy.","whyWrong":["It holds the new value.","Only top-level properties are independent.","Nothing is frozen, and the write succeeds.",""]},{"q":"Which is the safest way to test whether an object has its own property `name`?","options":["obj.hasOwnProperty('name')","obj.name !== undefined","Object.hasOwn(obj, 'name')","'name' in obj"],"answer":2,"why":"in searches the whole prototype chain, and a property explicitly set to undefined defeats a plain !== undefined check.","whyWrong":["Works, but breaks on objects created with Object.create(null).","A property set to undefined reads the same as an absent one.","","It includes inherited properties."]}],"js11":[{"q":"`[10, 9, 1].sort()` returns what?","options":["[1, 9, 10]","[10, 9, 1]","[9, 10, 1]","[1, 10, 9]"],"answer":3,"why":"sort converts to strings by default, so \"1\" sorts before \"9\". Pass a comparator: (a, b) => a - b.","whyWrong":["That needs a numeric comparator.","sort does change the order.","Not a lexicographic ordering either.",""]},{"q":"Why must `reduce` be given an initial value?","options":["It makes reduce faster, since the engine can preallocate knowing the accumulator's shape","It sets the return type, since without one the accumulator inherits the type of the first element","It is required syntax, and omitting it is a parse error rather than a runtime consideration","On an empty array without one it throws, and with mixed types it starts from element zero"],"answer":3,"why":"The empty case is the one that appears in production and not in your test data.","whyWrong":["Performance is unaffected.","There are no types to set.","It is optional syntactically, which is the problem.",""]},{"q":"Which method mutates the array it is called on?","options":["map","sort","slice","filter"],"answer":1,"why":"sort and reverse mutate; toSorted and toReversed are the copying versions. Spread into a new array first if the caller's data matters.","whyWrong":["Returns a new array.","","Returns a new array.","Returns a new array."]}],"js12":[{"q":"`{ ...defaults, ...options }`, which object wins on a conflicting key?","options":["options, because it is spread last","Neither (it throws)","They are merged deeply","defaults, because it is first"],"answer":0,"why":"Per-key override by presence, which is why an explicit port of 0 survives here but would be lost with a || fallback.","whyWrong":["","Duplicate keys are legal in a literal.","Spread is shallow.","Later spreads overwrite earlier ones."]},{"q":"What does `const { password, ...safe } = user` do?","options":["Throws a TypeError when password is absent from user","Pulls password out and collects everything else into a new object","Deletes the password key from the user object and returns what is left","Renames the password property to safe on the same object"],"answer":1,"why":"The clean way to drop a field without mutating the original, unlike delete, which changes the caller's object.","whyWrong":["A missing key destructures to undefined without error.","","The original object is untouched; a new one is built.","Renaming would be written password: safe, and rest is not that."]},{"q":"Three dots on the LEFT of an assignment or in a parameter list does what?","options":["Collects, it is rest","Nothing; it is only valid on the right","Both, depending on the type","Expands (it is spread)"],"answer":0,"why":"Same syntax, opposite jobs, told apart by position: left collects, right expands.","whyWrong":["","It is valid in both places.","Position decides, not type.","That is its behavior on the right."]}],"js13":[{"q":"Why choose a `Map` over a plain object for a lookup table built from user input?","options":["Maps serialize to JSON automatically","Objects cannot hold more than 1000 keys","Keys keep their type and cannot reach the prototype","Maps are always faster for string keys"],"answer":2,"why":"A user-supplied key of \"__proto__\" or \"constructor\" behaves strangely on a plain object.","whyWrong":["A Map stringifies to {}, the opposite.","There is no such limit.","","Speed depends on the workload; plain objects often win on fixed keys."]},{"q":"What does `JSON.stringify({ d: new Date(), u: undefined })` produce?","options":["It throws, because a Date cannot be serialized","Both values come out as null in the JSON string that is produced","Both survive unchanged and round-trip back cleanly","The date becomes a string and the undefined key disappears"],"answer":3,"why":"Dates, undefined, functions, Map, Set and NaN are all lost or altered, silently. This is why JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x)) is a bad deep-copy idiom.","whyWrong":["Dates serialize fine; a bigint is what throws.","NaN becomes null; undefined is dropped entirely.","Neither survives intact through a round trip.",""]},{"q":"What happens when `JSON.parse` receives malformed text?","options":["It throws a SyntaxError","It returns undefined","It returns null","It returns the input unchanged"],"answer":0,"why":"Unlike most of JavaScript, this is an exception rather than a quiet undefined, so anything parsed from a network response or user input needs a try/catch.","whyWrong":["","It does not return at all on failure.","That is what a safe wrapper returns after catching.","No."]}],"js14":[{"q":"How should a stack trace be read?","options":["Top down: the first line is where it broke, each line below is the caller","Alphabetically, since the frames are sorted by function name","Bottom up, since the last line is where the throw happened","Only the last line matters, since everything above it is just framework noise"],"answer":0,"why":"The first line in YOUR code is usually where to look, even when the top frames are inside a library.","whyWrong":["","Frames appear in call order. Nothing is sorted by name.","The bottom frame is the outermost caller, usually your entry point.","The whole chain is the useful part. It shows how you got there."]},{"q":"A function's scope chain is decided by...","options":["Where it is called from","Whether it is an arrow function","Where it is written in the source","The order files are loaded"],"answer":2,"why":"That is lexical scoping, and it is why closures work. Contrast with `this`, which IS decided by the call.","whyWrong":["That is how `this` is decided, not scope.","Arrows differ on this, not on the scope chain.","","Irrelevant to scope."]},{"q":"`RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded` most often means what?","options":["A number exceeded MAX_SAFE_INTEGER","The heap ran out of room for new objects","Your data was genuinely too deep to walk","Infinite recursion, a missing or unreachable base case"],"answer":3,"why":"Genuinely deep data does happen, but a logic error is far more common. The stack is finite in every engine.","whyWrong":["That is a precision problem, not a stack one.","That is OutOfMemory, a different error.","Possible, and the rarer explanation.",""]}],"js15":[{"q":"What exactly does a closure capture?","options":["A copy of the value at creation time","The binding, so it sees later changes to the variable","Only variables it names explicitly, by value","The whole call stack"],"answer":1,"why":"The var-loop bug proves it: three callbacks kept a reference to one variable and read it when they finally ran.","whyWrong":["If it copied values, the var loop would print 0, 1, 2.","","It captures bindings, not values.","Only the scope chain."]},{"q":"Why does each call to `makeCounter()` produce an independent count?","options":["The count is stored on the prototype of the function that gets returned each time","Counters are kept in a per-module store the runtime owns","Every call creates a fresh scope for the returned function to close over","JavaScript copies the function body afresh on every call"],"answer":2,"why":"That is the mechanism behind almost every practical use of closures.","whyWrong":["It is a local variable, not a property on anything.","There is no such per-module store in JavaScript.","","The function is not what holds the state; the scope is."]},{"q":"What is the memory cost of a closure?","options":["It roughly doubles the size of the function object itself","It keeps its whole enclosing scope alive, not just the variables it uses","It prevents the function from ever being garbage collected","There is none, since the enclosing scope is freed as soon as the call returns"],"answer":1,"why":"Usually irrelevant, and occasionally a leak: a handler capturing a large array and registered on a long-lived emitter keeps that array reachable forever.","whyWrong":["The function object is the same size either way.","","It is collected as soon as nothing references it.","The captured scope survives the call, and that is the cost."]}],"js16":[{"q":"`const g = user.greet; g();` throws. Why?","options":["this is bound by the call, and there was no object left of the dot","Methods cannot be assigned to a plain variable and called later","Strict mode forbids calling a method that has been detached from its object","greet was defined with shorthand syntax rather than the function keyword"],"answer":0,"why":"The most common this bug, and it appears wherever a method is passed as a callback.","whyWrong":["","They can be, the binding is what is lost.","Strict mode makes this undefined rather than the global, which is why it throws loudly.","The declaration form is irrelevant."]},{"q":"Which rule wins when a function is called with `new` AND was previously bound?","options":["bind","It throws","new","Whichever came last in the source"],"answer":2,"why":"The precedence is new, then explicit binding, then the method call, then the plain call, with arrows outside the system entirely.","whyWrong":["bind loses to new.","It is legal.","","Source order is irrelevant."]},{"q":"Why is an arrow function the wrong choice for an object method?","options":["It inherits this from where it was defined, not from the object","Arrows are not allowed as property values in an object literal","Arrows cannot return a value unless you write an explicit return","this is always undefined inside an arrow function body"],"answer":0,"why":"At module level that means it captures the module's this, so obj.method() returns undefined rather than the property.","whyWrong":["","They can appear there, they just misbehave.","A concise arrow body returns its expression with no return keyword.","It inherits one, which is usually wrong rather than absent."]}],"js17":[{"q":"What are the two mandatory parts of a recursive function?","options":["A memo table and an accumulator argument","A base case, and a recursive case moving toward it","try/catch and a depth counter","A loop and a return statement"],"answer":1,"why":"Missing either gives you RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded.","whyWrong":["Both are optimizations, not requirements.","","Useful sometimes, not mandatory.","Recursion is what replaces the loop."]},{"q":"Naive recursive `fib(40)` is slow. What fixes it without rewriting the algorithm?","options":["Increasing the stack size","Memoising, usually with a cache in a closure","Tail-call optimization","Converting it to an arrow function"],"answer":1,"why":"The problem is recomputing the same values exponentially many times, not the depth.","whyWrong":["Depth is not the problem here.","","In the spec, but implemented essentially only by Safari.","Syntax changes nothing."]},{"q":"When must recursion be converted to an iterative version with an explicit stack?","options":["When the depth is unbounded, such as untrusted nested JSON","Never, since engines grow the stack on demand","When the function is asynchronous","Whenever the input is an array rather than an object"],"answer":0,"why":"Engines allow roughly 10,000 frames and the exact number varies, so deep recursion cannot be relied on in JavaScript at all.","whyWrong":["","The call stack has a fixed limit; it does not grow to fit.","Async is a separate concern.","Shape does not matter, only how deep the nesting goes."]}],"js18":[{"q":"What does `console.log('1'); setTimeout(()=>console.log('2'),0); Promise.resolve().then(()=>console.log('3')); console.log('4');` print?","options":["1, 4, 2, 3","1, 2, 3, 4","1, 3, 4, 2","1, 4, 3, 2"],"answer":3,"why":"Synchronous first, then the microtask queue drains completely, then the next macrotask, even with a delay of 0.","whyWrong":["Microtasks drain before any macrotask.","Timers never run before synchronous code.","3 cannot run before the synchronous 4.",""]},{"q":"JavaScript is single-threaded, so how does a browser stay responsive during a download?","options":["The download is chunked into microtasks","The engine forks a second thread for I/O","Promises run their work in parallel","The host waits outside your thread and queues a callback"],"answer":3,"why":"Nothing in your code runs in parallel, the waiting happens elsewhere and the event loop delivers the result.","whyWrong":["No such chunking.","The engine has one thread; the runtime is what is not single-threaded.","A promise only reports a result; it starts no concurrency.",""]},{"q":"Why does wrapping a busy loop in a Promise not stop it blocking?","options":["The loop needs to sit inside an async function to yield properly","Async means not holding the stack while waiting, and CPU work holds it","It does stop blocking, since the executor runs on a separate task","Promises run synchronously, so wrapping anything is pointless"],"answer":1,"why":"CPU-heavy work belongs in a worker, not in a promise.","whyWrong":["async marks how a function returns. It does not make a loop yield.","","The executor runs synchronously on the same stack, right where you called it.","Promise callbacks run as microtasks. Only the executor body is synchronous."]}],"js19":[{"q":"Why can a callback not `throw` back to its caller?","options":["Callbacks run in a separate thread","By the time it runs, the caller's stack frame is gone","It can, if the caller used try/catch","throw is disabled inside callbacks"],"answer":1,"why":"That single fact is why the error-first convention exists, and why try/catch cannot cross an async boundary.","whyWrong":["Same thread, later stack.","","The try block has already exited.","throw works (it just goes nowhere useful.)"]},{"q":"A function calls back synchronously on a cache hit and asynchronously on a miss. Why is that a problem?","options":["It cannot be wrapped in a promise","Two orderings, so a bug appears on only one path","It leaks memory on every cache hit","Caches must always be synchronous"],"answer":1,"why":"Promise callbacks are always asynchronous, which removes the whole class, this hazard is nicknamed Zalgo.","whyWrong":["It can be wrapped, and wrapping is the usual fix.","","Nothing is retained; memory is not the issue.","They need not be."]},{"q":"When is a callback still the right shape rather than a promise?","options":["For anything that might fail, since promises hide errors","Never, promises cover every case in modern code","For anything involving I/O","For things that happen many times, not once"],"answer":3,"why":"A promise represents ONE future value, so it is the wrong shape for a click handler.","whyWrong":["Promises carry failure well; a rejection is not hidden.","Every event listener is a callback, and promises cannot replace one.","Promises handle I/O well.",""]}],"js20":[{"q":"What is the difference between `thenApply`-style `.then` returning a value and returning a promise?","options":["There is no difference","Returning a promise nests it two levels deep","Returning a value skips the next .then","Returning a promise makes the chain wait and unwrap it"],"answer":3,"why":"Forgetting to RETURN inside a then is the classic promise bug: the chain does not wait and the next step gets undefined.","whyWrong":["The behavior differs materially.","then flattens automatically (that is thenCompose)'s job in other languages.","The value is passed on.",""]},{"q":"You need every result and any failure is fatal. Which combinator?","options":["Promise.race","Promise.all","Promise.allSettled","Promise.any"],"answer":1,"why":"all rejects immediately on the first failure, and note the others keep running, because promises cannot be canceled.","whyWrong":["First to settle either way.","","Never rejects; use it when partial success is useful.","First to fulfill."]},{"q":"What happens to a rejected promise you neither await nor catch, in Node 15 and later?","options":["It throws at the next await","It terminates the process as an unhandled rejection","It is ignored once the promise is garbage collected","It is retried once before Node gives up"],"answer":1,"why":"The correct default, a program in an unknown state should stop, but it means a forgotten await is now a crash.","whyWrong":["There is no later await to throw at.","","Node reports it instead of ignoring it, and has since v15.","Nothing retries a settled promise."]}],"js21":[{"q":"Three independent fetches each take 1 second. Awaiting them on separate lines takes how long?","options":["It depends on how the server handles concurrent requests","3 seconds, each await waits before the next request starts","1 second, since the runtime starts all three in parallel","2 seconds, the first two overlap and the third follows"],"answer":1,"why":"Promise.all starts all three and waits once. This is the most common async review comment there is.","whyWrong":["The pattern decides this, not the server. The requests never overlap.","","That is what Promise.all gives you, and only if you ask for it.","Nothing overlaps here. Each await blocks the next call."]},{"q":"`ids.forEach(async id => { await save(id); })`, what goes wrong?","options":["It saves each id twice, once for the callback and once for the promise","It throws a SyntaxError, since async callbacks are not allowed in forEach","forEach discards the returned promises, so nothing is awaited and errors vanish","Nothing, forEach awaits each callback before moving to the next id"],"answer":2,"why":"Use for...of to sequence, or Promise.all with map to run concurrently.","whyWrong":["Each id is saved once. The problem is that nobody waits for the save.","An async callback is valid syntax there, which is why the bug survives review.","","forEach ignores the returned promise and starts the next iteration immediately."]},{"q":"Why is a forgotten `await` on a boolean check especially dangerous?","options":["It throws a TypeError when a Promise is used in a condition","A Promise object is always truthy, so the check passes unconditionally","await is optional when the function returns a plain boolean","The condition comes out false every time, so the guarded branch never runs"],"answer":1,"why":"`if (isAdmin(u))` without await is a security check that never fails, silently.","whyWrong":["No error is raised. A Promise in a condition is perfectly legal.","","An async function always returns a Promise, so await is never optional.","It is the opposite. A Promise is truthy, so the branch always runs."]}],"js22":[{"q":"How does the engine resolve a property that is not on the object itself?","options":["It throws a ReferenceError immediately","It returns null once the object has no such key","It searches every object in memory","It follows the prototype link upward until null"],"answer":3,"why":"Reading searches upward; missing at the end of the chain gives undefined.","whyWrong":["Reading an absent property is legal and returns a value.","A missing property reads as undefined, never null.","Only the chain.",""]},{"q":"`dog.speak = () => 'woof'` when speak came from the prototype. What changed?","options":["An own property on dog now shadows the prototype","The prototype's speak was overwritten","Every object sharing that prototype now says woof","It throws, prototype methods are read-only"],"answer":0,"why":"Writing always lands on the object itself, which is why one object cannot corrupt its prototype by assignment.","whyWrong":["","Reading follows the chain; writing does not.","Only dog changed.","Assignment is allowed and simply creates an own property."]},{"q":"Why should a lookup table built from untrusted JSON use `Object.create(null)` or a Map?","options":["Plain objects cannot hold more than a few thousand keys","Keys taken from JSON have to be converted to symbols","Object.create(null) is required before you can nest one object in another","A key of __proto__ can otherwise pollute every object in the program"],"answer":3,"why":"Prototype pollution is a real vulnerability class, not a theoretical one.","whyWrong":["Object key counts are not capped, and speed is not the reason anyway.","JSON keys are strings and stay strings.","Nesting works the same either way.",""]}],"js23":[{"q":"`class` in JavaScript is...","options":["A construct that exists only at compile time","Syntax over prototypes (methods still land on the prototype)","A genuinely new object model added in ES2015","An alias for Object.create with a nicer name"],"answer":1,"why":"Which is why typeof a class is \"function\", and why knowing prototypes is what lets you debug class behavior.","whyWrong":["It exists at runtime.","","Nothing new was added to the object model.","Related, not an alias."]},{"q":"What is genuinely new about `#private` fields, versus the old underscore convention?","options":["They are faster to look up than properties","They cannot be reached from outside the class","They are omitted from JSON","They cannot be reassigned"],"answer":1,"why":"The underscore was a request; # is a rule enforced by the language.","whyWrong":["Access speed is not what changed.","","A side effect, not the purpose.","They are mutable within the class."]},{"q":"A class field written as an arrow (`handler = () => {}`) rather than a method, what does it cost?","options":["It cannot see this, so the handler needs an explicit bind","A new function per instance, and a subclass cannot override it normally","It has to be declared static to sit on the class body","Nothing, the engine hoists the function onto the prototype"],"answer":1,"why":"Its one real use is a handler you must detach from the object and pass elsewhere without losing this.","whyWrong":["It captures this permanently, which is the whole reason people write it that way.","","Instance fields are fine. static only changes what it is attached to.","Nothing is hoisted. Each instance gets its own function object."]}],"js24":[{"q":"Why must `super(...)` come before any use of `this` in a subclass constructor?","options":["The parent constructor is what creates the object","Otherwise the prototype chain is reversed","So the subclass can be extended further","It is a style convention"],"answer":0,"why":"Touching this first is a ReferenceError, and omitting super() entirely is the same error.","whyWrong":["","The chain is unaffected.","Unrelated.","It is enforced by the language."]},{"q":"`class Stack extends Array`, what is the problem?","options":["Stack inherits the entire Array surface, including methods that break stack semantics","It is slower than composition, because engines deoptimize element access on subclassed arrays","Nothing, extending Array is the normal way to build a stack in modern JavaScript","Arrays cannot be extended, since Array is an exotic object that class syntax refuses to subclass"],"answer":0,"why":"Inheritance couples you to everything the parent has, forever. Composition lets you expose a deliberate surface.","whyWrong":["","Performance is not the argument.","There is a real design problem.","They can be."]},{"q":"When is inheritance justified over composition?","options":["Whenever there is shared behavior to reuse","Whenever two classes have a common field","When the subclass can stand in for the parent anywhere","Whenever the hierarchy is already deep"],"answer":2,"why":"That substitution constraint is what makes inheritance safe, and it is violated more often than honored.","whyWrong":["Shared behavior alone argues for composition.","A shared field is not an is-a relationship.","","Depth argues against another level."]}],"js25":[{"q":"What makes an object usable with `for...of` and spread?","options":["An entries() method returning key/value pairs","A [Symbol.iterator] method returning an iterator","Being an instance of Array or a subclass of it","A length property and numeric keys"],"answer":1,"why":"Iteration is a protocol, and implementing it makes your own types work with the language constructs.","whyWrong":["Helpful, but not the protocol.","","Not required (Map and Set are not arrays.)","Array-like, but not iterable."]},{"q":"What is the practical advantage of a generator over a hand-written iterator?","options":["A generator can be awaited directly in an async function","Far less machinery, and it is lazy, values are produced on demand","Iterating one is faster than iterating a hand-written iterator object","Any plain object becomes iterable without extra work"],"answer":1,"why":"Laziness lets you express an infinite or enormous sequence without materialising it.","whyWrong":["That is an async generator, consumed with for await.","","Not generally faster; the win is the code you do not write.","You still have to add a Symbol.iterator method yourself."]},{"q":"Why are plain objects not iterable by default?","options":["It is ambiguous whether you mean keys, values or entries","It would break JSON.stringify on plain objects","Objects have no defined key order to iterate","They became iterable in ES2020"],"answer":0,"why":"So you say which: Object.keys, Object.values or Object.entries.","whyWrong":["","Iteration and stringify are independent; nothing would break.","String keys keep insertion order, and integer-like keys come first.","No release made plain objects iterable; they still are not."]}],"js26":[{"q":"Why is `throw new Error('x')` better than `throw 'x'`?","options":["It is faster: allocating an Error object costs more","Strict mode requires the operand of throw to be an object","A bare string cannot be thrown, so the engine wraps it in an Error","A string loses the stack trace, the name and any instanceof check"],"answer":3,"why":"Throwing a non-Error is legal and costs you every piece of diagnostic information.","whyWrong":["Performance is irrelevant here, and the cost is negligible anyway.","Strict mode accepts either form; both are legal.","The engine throws the string exactly as given, and that is the trap.",""]},{"q":"You catch an error and throw a new one. What must you pass to keep the evidence?","options":["The original message, concatenated","e.stack as a string property","{ cause: e } as the second Error argument","Nothing, it is preserved automatically"],"answer":2,"why":"Error.cause keeps the original error AND its stack. Throwing a fresh error without it destroys the only record of what happened.","whyWrong":["The message is not the stack.","Manual and lossy.","","It is not."]},{"q":"Why must a custom error class set `this.name` explicitly?","options":["It sets the HTTP status the framework returns for that error","name is inherited, so the subclass reports itself as Error in logs","Without it, instanceof fails against the custom class","Without it, the constructor throws when super is called"],"answer":1,"why":"One line, and it is the difference between a useful log and a generic one.","whyWrong":["Nothing maps name to a status. Your handler does that itself.","","instanceof follows the prototype chain and works either way.","The constructor runs fine. You just get a uselessly generic name."]}],"js27":[{"q":"What does a `return` inside `finally` do to an in-flight exception?","options":["It discards it silently and returns the value instead","It re-throws after the finally block returns","Nothing, the exception still propagates to the caller","It is a SyntaxError in strict mode"],"answer":0,"why":"Use finally for cleanup only, and put the return after the whole try/catch/finally.","whyWrong":["","The exception is gone.","The return overrides it.","The syntax is legal in both strict and sloppy mode."]},{"q":"What is wrong with a catch block that logs and continues?","options":["A catch block cannot call console.log without rethrowing first","Logging in a catch block blocks the event loop until the write lands","Nothing, a logged error has been handled by definition","It removes information without recovering, the failure becomes invisible"],"answer":3,"why":"If you cannot say what the handler does about the failure, let the error travel to somewhere that can.","whyWrong":["It can log freely, and rethrowing is a separate decision.","console.log is not a blocking disk write here, and speed is not the problem.","Logging records the failure. It does not deal with it.",""]},{"q":"Where should errors generally be caught?","options":["Never, let every error propagate to the top","Only in main(), at the top of the program","At boundaries where a failure has a defined response","In every function that can fail"],"answer":2,"why":"A request handler returns a 500, a job retries, a CLI exits non-zero. Deep utilities should throw and say why.","whyWrong":["Something must eventually respond.","Too coarse for a server.","","That produces handlers that cannot recover."]}],"js28":[{"q":"Why does a `try`/`catch` around `setTimeout` not catch a throw from inside the callback?","options":["It does catch it, because the timer callback keeps the enclosing scope alive","catch only handles synchronous errors raised by built-in code","setTimeout swallows any error its callback happens to throw","The callback runs later on a fresh, empty stack (the try block has exited)"],"answer":3,"why":"You cannot try/catch across an asynchronous boundary. Promises exist largely to give errors a path back.","whyWrong":["Scope is preserved, the call stack is not, and catch needs the stack.","catch takes any throw on its own stack, whatever raised it.","Nothing is swallowed. The error reaches the global handler instead.",""]},{"q":"Which of these produces an error that ESCAPES your handling?","options":["An awaited call sitting inside a try/catch block","A promise chain that ends with a .catch handler","An async function calling doWork() with no await and no .catch","A plain synchronous throw from inside the same try block as the call"],"answer":2,"why":"The rejection has nowhere to go, and since Node 15 that terminates the process.","whyWrong":["await delivers the rejection back to your stack, so catch sees it.","That is an explicit handler, so nothing escapes.","","Same stack as the try, so the catch runs."]},{"q":"What should `process.on('uncaughtException')` do?","options":["Log and continue serving","Log and exit the process","Restart the event loop","Retry the failed operation"],"answer":1,"why":"Continuing risks corrupting data in ways far worse than a restart.","whyWrong":["The state is not trustworthy.","","Not a thing you can do.","There is no context to retry from."]}],"js29":[{"q":"`Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'city')` means what?","options":["city is not a valid property name","city was undefined","The thing you read city FROM was undefined","The object is null"],"answer":2,"why":"That distinction points at a different line, and misreading it is the single most common wasted hour in JavaScript.","whyWrong":["Any string is valid.","Reading an undefined property does not throw.","","null gives a slightly different message."]},{"q":"You have no hypothesis at all about a bug. What is the technique?","options":["Upgrade the dependencies","Add logs everywhere","Rewrite the suspicious function","Bisect, halve the problem space and repeat"],"answer":3,"why":"git bisect finds the breaking commit among a thousand in about ten steps, because halving is a base-2 logarithm.","whyWrong":["Unrelated to finding the cause.","Scattershot, and it changes timing.","Changing code without a hypothesis is guessing.",""]},{"q":"Which is better than `console.log(obj)` when you need to see full nesting?","options":["console.table(obj)","console.trace()","console.log(`obj: ${obj}`)","console.dir(obj, { depth: null })"],"answer":3,"why":"And never interpolate an object into a template literal, you get \"[object Object]\".","whyWrong":["Best for arrays of objects.","Shows how you got here, not the value.","That is the useless form.",""]}],"js30":[{"q":"Why does a breakpoint beat a console.log?","options":["It is faster to type, and the browser strips it from the shipped bundle","It shows every variable in scope, including ones you did not think to log","It survives a page reload, so the paused state comes back where you left it","It works in production, where you cannot attach a console"],"answer":1,"why":"And you can walk the call stack from there to see how execution arrived.","whyWrong":["A breakpoint never enters your bundle, so there is nothing to strip.","","The breakpoint persists, the paused frame does not. A reload starts execution over.","The console is there in production too, and remote logging is what usually wins."]},{"q":"You need to inspect one specific record in a loop over ten thousand. Which breakpoint?","options":["A logpoint","Pause on exceptions","A conditional breakpoint","A DOM breakpoint"],"answer":2,"why":"Right-click the line number and give it an expression such as user.id === 4172.","whyWrong":["That logs and continues, useful, but it will not pause on the one you want.","For a throw, not a value.",""," For element changes."]},{"q":"You want to know what code triggered a particular network request. What do you use?","options":["The Console, which prints the calling function for each request","A DOM breakpoint, since every fetch updates the document when it lands","An XHR/fetch breakpoint, or the Initiator column in the Network panel","Pause on caught exceptions, which the network stack raises per request"],"answer":2,"why":"Both give you the call stack at the moment the request was about to be sent.","whyWrong":["The Console prints what you log. It does not attribute requests to callers.","DOM breakpoints fire on node changes, and plenty of fetches never touch the DOM.","","A normal request throws nothing, so the debugger never pauses."]}],"js31":[{"q":"Which Scope group shows you variables you cannot reach any other way?","options":["Global","Local","Closure","Script"],"answer":2,"why":"It makes closures concrete, you can watch the captured binding change between iterations.","whyWrong":["Available by name.","Available by name anyway.","","Available by name."]},{"q":"A stack trace is fifteen frames of React and lodash before your own code. What fixes that?","options":["Use console.trace instead","Add the library scripts to the ignore list","Reduce the stack size shown in DevTools","Turn off source maps for the bundle"],"answer":1,"why":"Stepping then steps THROUGH library code instead of into it. It is the highest-value DevTools setting for framework work, and it is off by default.","whyWrong":["Same frames.","","There is no such setting, and fewer frames would hide your own code.","That leaves you reading minified frames instead."]},{"q":"Breakpoints do not bind and variables are single letters. What is the likely cause?","options":["DevTools is out of date in this browser","The source map is missing, unreachable or stale","The code is minified beyond recovery","The bundle is cached by the browser"],"answer":1,"why":"Check the Console for source-map warnings and the sourceMappingURL comment at the end of the bundle.","whyWrong":["Rarely the cause.","","That is what a source map recovers.","A cache would show old code, not single letters."]}],"js32":[{"q":"Why must Preserve log be enabled before tracing a redirect flow?","options":["It disables the HTTP cache so every hop is fetched fresh","It records twice as much detail for each captured request","It switches on the timing breakdown for every request the panel captures","Each navigation clears the panel, so you would see only the final hop"],"answer":3,"why":"This is the single reason most people find OAuth flows impossible to debug.","whyWrong":["Cache control is its own checkbox and does nothing for the log.","The detail per request is exactly the same either way.","Timing is recorded whether the box is checked or not.",""]},{"q":"The Console reports a CORS error. Where is the fix?","options":["In the browser settings","In your fetch options, set mode","In the DNS configuration for the API","On the server, it must send the CORS headers"],"answer":3,"why":"The request usually succeeded; the browser refused to let your JavaScript read the response. Changing fetch options changes which rules apply, not whether they are enforced.","whyWrong":["Not a user setting.","That changes the request, not the permission.","Unrelated.",""]},{"q":"What is the difference between a 401 and a 403?","options":["401 means no valid credential arrived; 403 means it did and lacks permission","403 means the requested resource does not exist, so the path itself must be wrong","They are interchangeable, most servers pick one at random","401 belongs to APIs and 403 to ordinary browser page requests"],"answer":0,"why":"They lead to completely different investigations, which is why collapsing them wastes time.","whyWrong":["","A missing resource is 404. A 403 says you are known and refused.","They carry different meanings and lead to different investigations.","Both apply anywhere. The distinction is about credentials, not clients."]}],"js33":[{"q":"A login flow works in incognito but not normally. What should you check first?","options":["Stale storage or a stale cookie, clear storage","Your network speed on the login request","Whether the JavaScript bundle failed to parse","The server logs for that one account"],"answer":0,"why":"Five seconds in the Application panel settles the question before you debug anything else.","whyWrong":["","Unrelated.","A parse failure would break the flow in incognito too.","Only after ruling out the client state."]},{"q":"`invalid_grant` at the token endpoint most often means what?","options":["The user was denied permission for the scope requested","The client secret sent to the token endpoint is wrong","The code was already used, expired, or the code_verifier does not match","The redirect_uri sent on the request was never registered for this client"],"answer":2,"why":"A double-mounted component redeeming the code twice is a common cause, an XHR breakpoint on /token catches it.","whyWrong":["A denial surfaces as access_denied or invalid_scope.","A bad secret reports as invalid_client, not invalid_grant.","","That fails earlier, at the authorize step, as redirect_uri_mismatch."]},{"q":"What is wrong with pasting a real id_token into an online decoder?","options":["It invalidates the token on the server","Nothing, the payload is public anyway","Online decoders cannot handle real tokens","It is a live credential, and you just gave it away"],"answer":3,"why":"Decode locally in the Console instead, and remember decoding is not verifying.","whyWrong":["The token stays valid, now in someone else's hands.","The payload is readable, but the signature is what makes it usable.","They can, which is the problem.",""]}],"js34":[{"q":"Why can you not write `if (cond) import { a } from './a.js'`?","options":["You can, the module loads only when cond is true","import is a reserved word in blocks","Only default imports may be conditional","Imports are static and resolved before any code runs"],"answer":3,"why":"Use the dynamic form, await import(), which returns a promise, that is how route-based code splitting works.","whyWrong":["The parser rejects it outright, so nothing loads either way.","It is the static structure, not the keyword.","Neither form may be.",""]},{"q":"Why prefer named exports over a default export?","options":["Named exports let a bundler split a file, which a default blocks","They load faster, since the runtime resolves them lazily","A typo becomes a build error rather than undefined, and the name stays greppable","Default exports are deprecated and warn under modern bundlers"],"answer":2,"why":"Each importer can rename a default to anything, which quietly makes a symbol impossible to find.","whyWrong":["Bundlers split on modules, not on export style. A default export is no barrier.","Resolution is the same either way, and nothing is deferred.","","They are not deprecated and nothing warns. They are just easy to rename."]},{"q":"Code that worked under a bundler fails in Node with a module-not-found error. What is the likely cause?","options":["A missing .js extension: Node requires it, bundlers historically did not","Node cannot load ESM at all unless a transpiler sits somewhere in the path","The package.json exports map is corrupt and resolves nothing","A circular import between two of the modules being loaded"],"answer":0,"why":"Browsers require the extension too, so the bundler was papering over it.","whyWrong":["","Node has run ESM natively for years.","A broken exports map fails the bundler too, and the bundler worked fine.","A cycle gives you an undefined binding, not a module-not-found error."]}],"js35":[{"q":"What decides whether a `.js` file is treated as ESM or CommonJS?","options":["The Node version you are running","The \"type\" field in the nearest package.json","The first line of the file, as a pragma","The presence of an import statement"],"answer":1,"why":"A .mjs or .cjs extension overrides it unconditionally. No type field means CommonJS, which is why 'Cannot use import statement outside a module' is the error people hit first.","whyWrong":["Version is unrelated.","","There is no pragma.","Node does not sniff the contents."]},{"q":"Why can CommonJS not be tree-shaken?","options":["It is too old, so bundlers deliberately skip the optimization rather than risk breaking it","Bundlers refuse to parse it, since the module format predates the tooling that performs the analysis","require is a function call that could take any string, so the graph is not statically known","It can be, provided every export is declared at the top level rather than assigned conditionally"],"answer":2,"why":"ESM's imports are resolved before execution, which is what lets tools drop unused exports.","whyWrong":["Age is not the reason.","They parse it fine.","","Not reliably."]},{"q":"What should a new Node project use?","options":["ESM only, unless it must support old consumers","Both, published as a dual package","CommonJS, for compatibility","Whichever the first dependency uses"],"answer":0,"why":"Dual-package publishing is one of the genuinely fiddly parts of the ecosystem, avoid it if you can.","whyWrong":["","Only if you must.","ESM is the standard and works everywhere now.","Your own modules need not follow."]}],"js36":[{"q":"What does `^4.19.2` allow?","options":["Any 4.19.x patch release","Any version at or above 4.19.2","Any 4.x at or above 4.19.2, but not 5.0.0","Exactly 4.19.2 and nothing else"],"answer":2,"why":"The caret never crosses a major, except for 0.x, where it treats the minor as breaking.","whyWrong":["That is the tilde.","It stops before the next major.","","That is a bare version."]},{"q":"What is the difference between `npm install` and `npm ci`?","options":["ci is faster, and that is the whole of the difference","install is for development, ci for production, otherwise identical","install resolves ranges and rewrites the lockfile; ci installs what is pinned","There is none, since both read the lockfile before resolving"],"answer":2,"why":"Running install in CI is how a build that passed yesterday fails today with a dependency nobody changed.","whyWrong":["It is faster, but determinism is the reason to use it.","The behavioral difference is what matters.","","install may rewrite the lockfile. ci refuses to touch it."]},{"q":"Why commit the lockfile even for a library?","options":["npm refuses to publish without it","Consumers need it to reproduce your install","It reduces the size of the published package","It makes YOUR builds reproducible, not your consumers'"],"answer":3,"why":"Consumers resolve their own tree, so your lockfile costs them nothing and buys you a reproducible CI.","whyWrong":["It does not.","They resolve their own tree from your version ranges.","The lockfile is not published, so package size is unchanged.",""]}],"js37":[{"q":"Browsers support modules natively. Why bundle at all?","options":["npm packages only publish bundled builds nowadays, so you have no real choice","To minify, since minifiers only run as part of a bundling step","Browsers only support modules behind a flag in most versions","Fewer requests, tree-shaking, non-JS assets, and bare specifier resolution"],"answer":3,"why":"In development the calculation reverses, which is why Vite serves unbundled ESM.","whyWrong":["Plenty of packages ship raw ESM, and npm has no say in how you build.","A minifier runs happily on a single file with no bundler in sight.","Native module support has been on by default for years.",""]},{"q":"What defeats tree-shaking?","options":["CommonJS dependencies and top-level side effects","Arrow functions in exported modules","Source maps generated in production","Exporting more names than you import"],"answer":0,"why":"The sideEffects: false field is how a library promises it has none so the bundler can drop unused modules.","whyWrong":["","Function style has no bearing on what the bundler can drop.","Source maps sit alongside the bundle and change nothing in it.","Unused exports are exactly what gets dropped."]},{"q":"What is the difference between a linter and a formatter?","options":["They are the same tool under two names in most toolchains","A formatter checks types while it rewrites the layout","A linter finds likely mistakes; a formatter just settles layout","A linter only checks style, which is why formatters replaced them"],"answer":2,"why":"Prettier's value is not its output, it is that it ends the discussion. Do not configure it.","whyWrong":["They do different jobs, and most projects run both.","That is a type checker. A formatter reads syntax and nothing more.","","Modern linters aim at correctness. The style rules moved to the formatter."]}],"js50":[{"q":"What happens to TypeScript type annotations when the code runs?","options":["They become runtime assertions","They are erased (there are no runtime type checks)","They stay as metadata","They are compiled into validation functions"],"answer":1,"why":"Which is why every boundary still needs a real runtime check.","whyWrong":["No checks are generated.","","Nothing survives.","Nothing is generated."]},{"q":"`const user = await res.json() as User`, what does the `as` do?","options":["Tells the compiler to stop checking; it verifies nothing","Converts the parsed object into a User at runtime","Throws at runtime if the shape is wrong","Validates the parsed shape against User"],"answer":0,"why":"A schema library validates at runtime AND produces the type, with no chance of the two drifting apart.","whyWrong":["","No conversion happens.","It cannot, it does not exist at runtime.","It is an assertion, not a check."]},{"q":"Which tsconfig setting matters most, and why?","options":["noEmit, since skipping output makes type checking much faster","target, because it controls which polyfills TypeScript emits","module, since it rewrites every import into a require call","strict, since strictNullChecks catches the commonest runtime error"],"answer":3,"why":"Adding it to an existing codebase later is genuinely painful, so turn it on from day one.","whyWrong":["Type checking does the same work either way. Emit is a small share of the time.","TypeScript emits no polyfills. target only picks the syntax level of the output.","module can emit ESM untouched. Nothing is rewritten unless you ask for CommonJS.",""]}],"js51":[{"q":"TypeScript uses structural typing. What does that mean?","options":["Two types only match when they are declared under the same name in both places","Any extra property on a value is always a compile error","Only a class can satisfy an interface, plain objects cannot","If the shape fits, it fits, nothing declares that it implements an interface"],"answer":3,"why":"It is what makes TypeScript feel natural over JavaScript, where nobody declares interfaces either.","whyWrong":["That is nominal typing, as in Java; TypeScript compares shapes.","Excess property checks fire only on object literals assigned directly.","Any object with the right shape satisfies it, class or not.",""]},{"q":"When should you reach for a generic?","options":["To make a function reusable","When the output type depends on the input type","Whenever a function takes an object","Whenever you would otherwise write any"],"answer":1,"why":"Writing T everywhere because it looks professional produces signatures nobody can read.","whyWrong":["Reuse does not require a type parameter.","","Not by itself.","unknown is usually the right answer there."]},{"q":"You need an API response type that is a User without its password field. Which utility type?","options":["Readonly<User>","Partial<User>","Pick<User, 'password'>","Omit<User, 'password'>"],"answer":3,"why":"Pick selects a subset; Omit removes one. Partial makes everything optional, which is for patch payloads.","whyWrong":["Prevents reassignment.","Makes every field optional.","Selects only password, the opposite.",""]}],"js52":[{"q":"Why is `unknown` better than `any` at a boundary?","options":["There is no difference, both accept any value that arrives at the boundary","any is deprecated and will be removed in a future release","unknown is faster to compile, since the checker can skip it","unknown keeps the checking on, you must prove the type before using it"],"answer":3,"why":"any disables checking for everything it touches and spreads through the codebase silently.","whyWrong":["Both accept any value, and only unknown makes you narrow it before use.","It is not deprecated, just dangerous, and it is not going anywhere.","Compile time is unaffected, and unknown means more checking, not less.",""]},{"q":"Which narrowing check works for distinguishing a Date from a string?","options":["Array.isArray","instanceof","the in operator","typeof"],"answer":1,"why":"typeof a Date is \"object\", the same as every other object, the same limitation as typeof null and typeof [].","whyWrong":["For arrays.","","For distinguishing object shapes by key.","Reports \"object\" for both Dates and plain objects."]},{"q":"What is the right way to add types to an existing JavaScript codebase?","options":["Incrementally: allowJs and checkJs first, then file by file","Rewrite the whole thing in TypeScript from scratch","Add any everywhere first, then refine the types file by file later","One big branch that converts every file at once"],"answer":0,"why":"A whole-codebase branch will not merge, and the types you write while fighting a rebase are not the ones you want.","whyWrong":["","That throws away working code and the bugs already fixed in it.","any spreads through the codebase and is hard to pull back out.","A branch that size will not merge, and the types suffer for it."]}],"jscap":[{"q":"Why must a link shortener validate the URL scheme with an allowlist?","options":["javascript: and data: parse as valid URLs, so 'did it parse?' lets them through","Blocklists are slower, since every new scheme adds another pattern to run on each call","To support internationalized domains, which need scheme-aware encoding","new URL already rejects any scheme the browser will not navigate to"],"answer":0,"why":"A stored javascript: URL later rendered as an href turns your shortener into an XSS delivery service.","whyWrong":["","Speed is not the problem. A blocklist fails because it cannot know tomorrow's schemes.","IDN handling lives in the host part. The scheme check has nothing to do with it.","new URL parses javascript: and data: happily. It only checks syntax."]},{"q":"In the store, why must the duplicate-URL check come BEFORE the capacity check?","options":["The Map itself throws once the store is at capacity","Otherwise a full store refuses to return a code it already has","The capacity check is the expensive one, so it has to be run last","Order does not matter, both checks are independent"],"answer":1,"why":"A repeat save adds nothing, so capacity is irrelevant to it, and the user sees the bug as 'my link stopped working'.","whyWrong":["A Map has no capacity and throws nothing.","","Both checks are trivial; cost is not the reason.","The order changes what a repeat save returns."]},{"q":"Every metric is bad at once: loop lag, error rate, latency and rejections. What do you investigate first?","options":["The highest number","The rate limiter, since it is cheapest to change","The error rate, since users see it","The blocked event loop (it inflates all the others)"],"answer":3,"why":"Diagnosis is an ordered list of causes, not a scoring function. Chasing a symptom first is how a twenty-minute fix becomes a two-day outage.","whyWrong":["Magnitude does not indicate causation.","Changing it would mask the real cause.","Errors here are downstream of the blockage.",""]}],"js38":[{"q":"What is Node.js, precisely?","options":["The V8 engine packaged with libraries for files, sockets and processes","A compiler that turns JavaScript into a native binary ahead of time","A dialect of JavaScript with a few extra syntax features","A browser build with the rendering engine switched off"],"answer":0,"why":"The language is identical; what changes is the surroundings, no DOM, and in its place fs, http, os and process.","whyWrong":["","It interprets and JITs at runtime, exactly like the browser does.","Same language, same syntax. Only the surrounding library changes.","There is no rendering engine in it at all, and no DOM."]},{"q":"Why is there no same-origin policy in Node?","options":["A browser sandboxes code it downloaded from a stranger; you chose to run this","There is one, applied to any fetch call made from a module","It is off by default and can be turned on with a runtime flag","Node predates the same-origin policy, so it never picked it up"],"answer":0,"why":"Which is exactly why installing an untrusted npm package is a serious act.","whyWrong":["","fetch in Node sends whatever Origin you set, and no policy blocks the response.","No such flag exists. There is nothing to enable.","The policy is far older than Node. Age is not the reason."]},{"q":"Which Node version should run in production?","options":["The newest release, always","An even-numbered LTS release","Whatever is on the machine","An odd-numbered release, for the features"],"answer":1,"why":"Pin it in three places that must agree: engines in package.json, the CI setup step, and the Docker base image.","whyWrong":["Odd majors are not supported long-term.","","That is how you get a syntax error the server cannot parse.","Odd releases are for trying features."]}],"js39":[{"q":"Which queue does Node drain FIRST between phases?","options":["process.nextTick, before promise microtasks","The promise microtask queue, before nextTick","The timers queue, since timers run first","The setImmediate queue in the check phase"],"answer":0,"why":"A recursive nextTick starves the loop completely, which is why queueMicrotask is usually the safer choice.","whyWrong":["","nextTick is drained first, ahead of promise microtasks.","Timers are a loop phase, not a between-phase queue.","The check phase runs later in the loop, not between phases."]},{"q":"Why is blocking worse in Node than in a browser?","options":["The V8 stack is smaller under Node","One loop serves every connected client","It is not worse, just more visible","Node runs without a JIT compiler"],"answer":1,"why":"A synchronous 200ms operation on a server handling 100 requests per second is not a slow endpoint, it is an outage.","whyWrong":["It is the same engine with the same stack limits.","","One blocked loop stalls every client, which is materially worse.","Node runs V8 with the same JIT the browser uses."]},{"q":"Which of these actually runs off the main thread?","options":["JSON.parse of a large string","A regex with catastrophic backtracking","Asynchronous file I/O, via the thread pool","A tight loop over a million rows"],"answer":2,"why":"Network I/O uses no pool at all, it is genuinely event-driven. CPU work belongs in worker_threads.","whyWrong":["Synchronous, and unavoidable, so bound the size.","Synchronous, and a real denial-of-service vector.","","Synchronous."]}],"js40":[{"q":"`process.env.DEBUG` is the string `\"false\"`. Is it truthy?","options":["Only in strict mode","Yes, every non-empty string is truthy","It is a boolean","No, the string false is falsy"],"answer":1,"why":"This turns feature flags on in production more often than any other mistake in configuration handling.","whyWrong":["Mode does not affect coercion.","","Everything in env is a string.","Only the eight falsy values are falsy."]},{"q":"Where should required environment variables be validated?","options":["In a try/catch wrapped around each read of process.env","At startup, exiting with a clear message if anything is missing","In the CI pipeline only, where the config is assembled","At the point of first use, right next to the code that needs the value"],"answer":1,"why":"Discovering a missing database URL when the first request arrives at 3am is strictly worse than failing to start.","whyWrong":["That defers the failure to whenever the read happens.","","CI cannot see production values.","Too late, and the checks end up scattered."]},{"q":"What is the risk of calling `process.exit()` directly?","options":["It restarts the process under a supervisor, keeping the same code","It ignores the exit code you pass and always reports zero","It is immediate, pending async work is abandoned and buffered output may be lost","The exit event never fires, so cleanup listeners are skipped entirely"],"answer":2,"why":"Let the loop drain, and use exit only after cleanup or on a fatal error.","whyWrong":["exit ends the process. Whether anything restarts it is your supervisor's business.","The code you pass is honored and reaches the parent process.","","The exit event does fire, though only synchronous work in its listeners completes."]}],"js41":[{"q":"When is `readFileSync` acceptable?","options":["At startup, before the server is listening","Never","Whenever the file is under 1MB","Inside a request handler for small files"],"answer":0,"why":"In a hot path it stops every connected client, and it shows up as the whole service being slow rather than one endpoint.","whyWrong":["","It is fine at startup.","Size does not change the blocking.","Never in a handler."]},{"q":"`await fs.readFile('a.txt')` with no encoding returns what?","options":["null","A string","An error","A Buffer of raw bytes"],"answer":3,"why":"String methods on a Buffer behave strangely rather than throwing, which makes it an annoying bug to spot.","whyWrong":["It succeeds.","Only with an encoding argument.","It succeeds.",""]},{"q":"Why is `if (await exists(p)) await readFile(p)` a bad pattern?","options":["The file can be deleted between the check and the read (a TOCTOU race)","It does not handle the case where the path is a directory","It is two round trips to the file system where one would do","fs.exists has been deprecated, so the call may vanish in a future release"],"answer":0,"why":"Attempt the operation and handle e.code === 'ENOENT' instead.","whyWrong":["","A separate concern, and the read fails clearly enough there.","True, and the cost is not what makes the pattern unsafe.","Deprecation is a symptom of this race, not the reason to avoid it."]}],"js42":[{"q":"Why is checking a user-supplied path for `\"..\"` not enough to stop path traversal?","options":["It blocks legitimate paths, so no valid request gets through","It is enough, provided the check runs before any decoding","Encoding, alternate separators and symlinks all bypass a substring check","It is too slow, since every request scans the whole string"],"answer":2,"why":"Resolve the path, then verify the result still starts with the base directory plus a separator.","whyWrong":["Plenty of valid paths contain no dots at all and pass the check fine.","Decode later and the payload still slips through as %2e%2e, or through a symlink.","","A substring scan costs nothing. Correctness is the failure here."]},{"q":"Why must the containment check compare against the base plus a separator?","options":["To handle Windows backslash separators","To make the comparison case-insensitive","Otherwise /uploads-evil passes a startsWith check","startsWith requires a trailing slash"],"answer":2,"why":"And the check needs an exact-match branch too, so the base directory itself is allowed.","whyWrong":["Separator style is a different problem from prefix matching.","Case folding is a separate concern and does not stop the prefix trick.","","It does not."]},{"q":"What removes the whole class of filename problems rather than filtering it?","options":["Generate your own name for the file on disk","Reject any name outside a strict ASCII allowlist","Escape the filename before joining the path","Lowercase the name and strip punctuation"],"answer":0,"why":"You never trust a filename, so do not use one as a path.","whyWrong":["","That is still filtering, and the name still becomes a path.","Escaping is filtering with extra steps.","Another filter, and it still trusts the user's name."]}],"js43":[{"q":"What problem does backpressure solve?","options":["Too many file descriptors held open by concurrent streams","A fast reader outpacing a slow writer, filling memory silently","Chunks arriving corrupted when a stream is piped too fast","Slow disks, by buffering writes until the device catches up"],"answer":1,"why":"Without coordination the unwritten data piles up and you have reinvented loading the whole file, less obviously.","whyWrong":["Descriptor limits are a separate problem with a separate fix.","","Speed does not corrupt data. The bytes arrive intact either way.","Buffering without a limit is the failure mode, not the fix."]},{"q":"Why use `pipeline` rather than `.pipe()`?","options":["It adds compression to the stream as it passes through","It is faster, since it moves chunks without an extra copy","It propagates errors and cleans up every stream if any fails","pipe has been formally deprecated in recent Node releases"],"answer":2,"why":".pipe leaks handles on error, which is precisely why pipeline exists.","whyWrong":["Compression only appears if you put a transform in the chain.","Throughput is the same. Both end up on the same write path.","","It is not deprecated, just easy to misuse when something fails."]},{"q":"Decoding each chunk of a stream separately can corrupt text. Why?","options":["A multi-byte character can span two chunks","It cannot, chunks land on character boundaries","The encoding changes mid-stream","Buffers are not safe to read twice"],"answer":0,"why":"setEncoding on the stream, or StringDecoder, hold partial characters back until they are complete.","whyWrong":["","Chunks are byte counts; nothing aligns them to characters.","It does not.","A Buffer can be read as often as you like."]}],"js44":[{"q":"What happens if a request handler never calls `res.end()`?","options":["Node sends a 500 automatically once the handler returns","The response is empty but complete, headers go out with a zero length body","The request hangs until the client times out, and the connection stays open","It throws once the server-side request timer expires"],"answer":2,"why":"Enough of them exhaust the server, which is why every path, including the error branches, must end the response exactly once.","whyWrong":["Node has no idea the handler finished. It waits for you to end the response.","Nothing is flushed at all. The client sees no headers and no body.","","There is no such timer firing here. The handler just goes quiet."]},{"q":"Why must you bound the size of a request body?","options":["One client can otherwise send an endless body and exhaust your memory for free","Node already caps request bodies at 1MB, so the check is redundant","So the server can put a correct Content-Length on its response","JSON.parse needs the length in advance to allocate its buffer"],"answer":0,"why":"And the check has to run inside the loop, so you stop at the limit rather than after buffering the whole payload.","whyWrong":["","There is no default cap in Node. A framework may add one, but nothing does by default.","Content-Length on the response has nothing to do with the incoming body.","JSON.parse takes a string and asks for no size hint."]},{"q":"`req.url` gives you what?","options":["The full absolute URL including the host","The path only, with the query stripped","The path and query only, not the scheme or host","The Host header value on its own"],"answer":2,"why":"Parse it with `new URL(req.url, 'http://' + req.headers.host)` rather than splitting on '?'.","whyWrong":["The host arrives separately, in the Host header.","The query string is included as-is.","","That is req.headers.host, a different field."]}],"js45":[{"q":"A path exists but not for this method. What status should you return?","options":["404, since this method and path pair does not exist","400, because the client sent a method the route rejects","405, with an Allow header listing the methods that do exist","403, because this method is not permitted on that route"],"answer":2,"why":"Returning 404 hides a client bug that 405 explains immediately.","whyWrong":["404 says the path is unknown, which sends the client hunting for a typo.","The request was well-formed. 400 is for a request the server cannot parse.","","403 is about who you are, not about which methods the route implements."]},{"q":"Why must CORS middleware run BEFORE authentication?","options":["The CORS check is cheaper, so it belongs first for speed","It must run after authentication so the origin is known","Authentication middleware needs the CORS headers to be set first","A preflight request carries no credentials by design"],"answer":3,"why":"Otherwise preflights are rejected, the browser never sends the real request, and you debug the wrong thing.","whyWrong":["Speed is irrelevant.","Before is correct, and the origin arrives in a header either way.","It does not.",""]},{"q":"Silently ignoring an unknown field like `{ role: 'admin' }` in a request body is which vulnerability?","options":["Cross-site scripting","Path traversal","Prototype pollution","Mass assignment"],"answer":3,"why":"Fine until someone spreads the body into a database write. Reject unknown fields rather than ignoring them.","whyWrong":["A rendering problem.","A file-system problem.","Related, but this is the assignment case.",""]}],"js46":[{"q":"Why compare an API key with `crypto.timingSafeEqual` rather than `===`?","options":["timingSafeEqual is faster than === on a long key comparison","=== compares string contents only up to the first 32 characters","There is no difference, both walk the same bytes in the same way","=== returns early, leaking through timing how many characters matched"],"answer":3,"why":"Enough attempts and an attacker forges the key one character at a time.","whyWrong":["It is slower on purpose, since it always reads every byte.","There is no such cutoff. It compares the whole value, just not in constant time.","One stops at the first mismatch, and that timing is the leak.",""]},{"q":"A 401 response is non-compliant without which header?","options":["Allow","Cache-Control","Retry-After","WWW-Authenticate"],"answer":3,"why":"It tells the client which scheme to use: Bearer, Basic, and so on.","whyWrong":["That belongs on 405.","Good practice, not required.","That belongs on 429 and 503.",""]},{"q":"Where should security headers be set?","options":["Once in middleware, applied to every response","On each route that needs them","In the HTML as meta tags","In the reverse proxy config only"],"answer":0,"why":"A header applied by hand is a header someone forgets on the endpoint that needed it most.","whyWrong":["","That is how one gets missed.","An API sends no HTML.","Useful as well, but the app should not depend on it."]}],"js47":[{"q":"You need to debug a script that finishes in 40 milliseconds. Which flag?","options":["kill -USR1 on the process once it starts","--inspect, then attach the debugger quickly","--trace-warnings, for the full stack trace on startup","--inspect-brk, which pauses before the first line"],"answer":3,"why":"With plain --inspect the process is gone before you can attach.","whyWrong":["That is for a process already running and waiting.","It runs immediately and exits before you can attach.","Warning traces are unrelated to pausing execution.",""]},{"q":"Why is `node --inspect=0.0.0.0:9229` in a container dangerous?","options":["It disables TLS on outbound calls","It leaks the heap to anyone scanning","It slows the process down noticeably","The inspector allows remote code execution"],"answer":3,"why":"Anyone who can reach that port can evaluate arbitrary code in your process. Bind to localhost and use an SSH tunnel.","whyWrong":["The inspector port does not touch your TLS settings.","Heap access needs an attached debugger, and that is the smaller problem.","Overhead is small until a debugger attaches.",""]},{"q":"What makes a log line useful during an incident?","options":["Structured JSON with a request id, so one request can be reassembled","A precise timestamp down to the microsecond on every line","A stack trace attached to every line so you always know the call path","Color coding by severity so the eye finds errors quickly"],"answer":0,"why":"Without the id, one request's story is lost among a thousand interleaved ones.","whyWrong":["","You need the timestamp, and it still will not say which lines belong together.","That is noise, and it is expensive to capture on every write.","Color is stripped the moment the line reaches your aggregator."]}],"js48":[{"q":"Why import from `node:assert/strict` rather than `node:assert`?","options":["Strict mode assertions run faster than the loose ones","The non-strict version compares with == and would say \"5\" equals 5","There is no difference, the strict entry point is an alias","The strict entry point adds assertion methods that the other one lacks"],"answer":1,"why":"Loose comparison in a test defeats the purpose of the test.","whyWrong":["They run at the same speed; the comparison is what differs.","","The difference is material: one compares loosely, one does not.","Same methods on both, with a different comparison underneath."]},{"q":"Which part of testing do people most often skip?","options":["Naming the tests descriptively","Testing the happy path end to end","Testing the failures, throws and rejects","Importing the assert module"],"answer":2,"why":"The failure paths are where the bugs are, and where the behavior is least obvious.","whyWrong":["Common, but less costly.","Nobody skips that.","","It would not run."]},{"q":"What should you mock?","options":["Nothing, real dependencies keep tests honest","Your own modules, so tests stay isolated","The boundary: the network, the clock, the file system","Every dependency the module imports"],"answer":2,"why":"Mocking your own modules couples the test to the structure of the code, which is exactly what you did not want.","whyWrong":["Some boundaries cannot be left real, the clock among them.","That is the trap.","","Mock everything and the test only checks your mocks."]}],"js49":[{"q":"How do you tell normal heap growth from a leak?","options":["A leak is heap that does not come back down after collection","Any growth at all counts as a leak worth chasing","A heapTotal above 100MB is the accepted leak threshold","Rising rss is the reliable signal, whatever the heap does"],"answer":0,"why":"Judge from the trend across several minutes, comparing the troughs rather than the peaks.","whyWrong":["","The heap expands normally between collections. Growth alone proves nothing.","Absolute size says nothing. A busy service sits far above that quite happily.","rss covers buffers, code and native memory, so it moves for many reasons."]},{"q":"Reading a CPU profile, what are you looking for?","options":["A deep stack","The first frame","The largest function","A wide frame, width is time spent"],"answer":3,"why":"A deep narrow stack is just a call chain; a wide plateau is where the milliseconds went.","whyWrong":["Depth is call nesting.","Position is not cost.","Size in lines is not cost.",""]},{"q":"Which single metric is most worth exporting from a Node service?","options":["Event loop lag","Request count","CPU percentage","Heap size"],"answer":0,"why":"It goes bad before latency does and points at a cause the request logs cannot show.","whyWrong":["","Says nothing about health.","Ambiguous on its own.","Useful, but slower to indicate trouble."]}],"jsdom1":[{"q":"What is the DOM?","options":["A live tree of objects the browser builds from your HTML","The text of your HTML file, kept in memory","A JavaScript language feature for templates","The part of the browser that draws pixels"],"answer":0,"why":"The browser parses your HTML once, builds a tree of objects from it, and from then on the page IS that tree: JavaScript changes the page by changing the tree.","whyWrong":["","The HTML text is only the starting recipe; editing it changes nothing after parsing.","It is a browser API, not part of the JavaScript language.","Painting happens after the tree changes; the DOM is the model, not the painter."]},{"q":"A user types a comment and your code inserts it into the page. Why is el.textContent the right tool rather than el.innerHTML?","options":["textContent is faster, and speed is the concern","innerHTML parses the string as markup and can run script","textContent preserves whitespace better","innerHTML only works on div elements"],"answer":1,"why":"innerHTML hands the string to the HTML parser, so \"<img onerror=...>\" in a comment executes. textContent treats every character as literal text. That difference is XSS.","whyWrong":["It is faster, but that is not the reason that matters here.","","Whitespace is not the issue, code execution is.","innerHTML works on any element."]},{"q":"You need to add 200 rows to a list. What is the well-shaped way to do it?","options":["Build the rows in a DocumentFragment, then append it once","Concatenate one giant HTML string of user data and set innerHTML","Add the rows one per setTimeout so the browser can keep up","Append each row to the document inside the loop"],"answer":0,"why":"A fragment is assembled off-page, so the document changes once instead of 200 times, one layout pass, and no string-built markup carrying injection risk.","whyWrong":["","Works until the data contains markup, that is the XSS pattern.","Spreading it over timers just spreads the same cost out.","Each append can trigger layout work; 200 of them is the classic slow loop."]}],"jsdom2":[{"q":"What is event bubbling?","options":["Parent handlers always run before child handlers","After firing on its target, an event travels up the ancestors","Events are queued until the page finishes loading","Events fire twice unless you stop propagation"],"answer":1,"why":"The event fires on the element you interacted with, then walks up parent by parent, which is what lets an ancestor hear about it at all.","whyWrong":["The target's handler runs first; ancestors hear it on the way up.","","Bubbling is about propagation through the tree, not timing.","It fires once; multiple listeners along the path may hear it."]},{"q":"A list gets new rows added at runtime, and clicks on every row must work. Why does one listener on the list (delegation) beat a listener per row?","options":["Because per-row listeners are removed automatically when rows change","Because browsers limit how many listeners an element can have","Because the list listener hears bubbled clicks from any row, present or future","Because event.target only works on the parent element that listens"],"answer":2,"why":"The parent listener catches clicks bubbling up from whatever rows exist at click time, rows added tomorrow included. event.target says which row it was.","whyWrong":["They are not removed automatically, and new rows never got one, which is the bug.","There is no such limit; the win is coverage of future rows.","","event.target works everywhere; it reports where the event started."]},{"q":"You click a button that sits inside a form, which sits inside a div, all three have click listeners. Who hears it?","options":["All three: the button first, then the form, then the div","Only the div, because it is outermost","All three, in whatever order the browser chooses","Only the button, since it was clicked"],"answer":0,"why":"The event fires on the target, then bubbles: button, form, div, inside out, in that order, every time.","whyWrong":["","The target hears it first, not the outermost.","The order is defined: target first, then up the tree.","The ancestors hear it too as it bubbles."]}],"jsdom3":[{"q":"fetch(url) returns a 404. What does the promise do?","options":["Resolves or rejects depending on the Content-Type","Throws synchronously","Rejects, because the request failed","Resolves, a 404 is a completed HTTP conversation"],"answer":3,"why":"fetch rejects only when no HTTP conversation happened at all: DNS failure, no network, CORS block. A 404 is a successful conversation with a negative answer, so it resolves.","whyWrong":["Content-Type affects parsing, not settlement.","Network work is never synchronous.","The transport succeeded; only the status is bad. That is why res.ok exists.",""]},{"q":"Why does well-written fetch code check res.ok before parsing the body?","options":["Because JSON.parse only works when the status is 200","Because parsing is slow and should be skipped when possible","Because res.ok retries the request when it is false","Because an error page parsed as JSON fails far from the cause"],"answer":3,"why":"Without the check, a 500 whose body is an HTML error page hits response.json() and fails with a parse error, pointing at the wrong culprit. Throwing on !res.ok names the real problem at the boundary.","whyWrong":["Parsing does not consult the status, that is exactly the problem.","Cost is not the point; correctness of the error is.","res.ok is just a boolean; it does nothing on its own.",""]},{"q":"The console shows a CORS error calling an API from your page. Where does the fix live?","options":["In your HTML, use a form instead of fetch","In the browser settings of each of your users","In your JavaScript (add the right request headers)","On the API's server, which must allow your origin"],"answer":3,"why":"CORS is the browser refusing to let your page read a cross-origin response unless the SERVER grants it. No client-side code can grant permission the server did not give.","whyWrong":["Dodging fetch dodges the protection, not the problem.","Users cannot and should not fix it.","Request headers cannot authorize your origin; only response headers can.",""]}],"jsdom4":[{"q":"What does new FormData(formElement) give you?","options":["A JSON string ready to POST","A validated copy of the form","The form's inner HTML as a string","Key/value pairs of every named field's value"],"answer":3,"why":"FormData reads every named control's current value in one line, the standard first step of a JavaScript-handled submit.","whyWrong":["It is not JSON; convert with Object.fromEntries(fd) if you need that.","It reads; validation is a separate, deliberate step.","It reads values, not markup.",""]},{"q":"The browser's built-in validation (required, type=\"email\") passes. Why must the server still validate the same data?","options":["Because HTML attributes expire after the page loads","Because anyone can send a request to your server without loading your form","Because browser validation only runs in Chrome and Safari","It does not need to, the client-side check is already sufficient"],"answer":1,"why":"The form is advice to honest browsers. curl, scripts and attackers talk to your server directly, so the server-side check is the only one that is actually enforced.","whyWrong":["Attributes do not expire; they just do not bind anyone.","","It runs in every modern browser (that is not the gap.)","Client-only validation is the classic vulnerability."]},{"q":"In a JavaScript-handled form, what does event.preventDefault() in the submit handler do?","options":["Blocks invalid values from entering the fields","Clears the form fields once submission finishes","Stops the browser's own navigate-and-reload submission","Disables the submit button until every field has validated"],"answer":2,"why":"The browser's default on submit is to serialize the form and navigate away. preventDefault cancels that navigation, leaving your fetch in charge of what happens next.","whyWrong":["It does not touch field values.","Nothing is cleared; the default action is simply canceled.","","Button state is your code's job; preventDefault does not touch it."]}],"jsdate":[{"q":"What is a JavaScript Date, underneath?","options":["A string in ISO format","A timezone-aware timestamp that remembers where it was created","A number of milliseconds since Jan 1 1970 UTC, with methods on it","A calendar object storing year, month and day fields"],"answer":2,"why":"A Date is just that one number. It has no timezone inside it, the timezone appears only when you FORMAT it for a human.","whyWrong":["Strings are one way in and out, not the representation.","It remembers no zone; toLocaleString applies the viewer's zone at display time.","","The fields are computed from the number on demand."]},{"q":"A user in New York saves their birthday as new Date(\"2026-03-03\") and the app later shows March 2nd. Why?","options":["getMonth is zero-based, so March reads as February and the day shifts back","Daylight saving time shifted the stored value back by a full day","A Date holds an offset from the moment it was created, so the value drifts as time passes","A date-only string parses as midnight UTC, which is the previous evening in New York"],"answer":3,"why":"\"2026-03-03\" means 2026-03-03T00:00:00Z. Midnight UTC is 7pm on March 2nd in New York, so formatting in local time shows the previous day. Store instants in full ISO form with the Z.","whyWrong":["Zero-based months change which month you read. They never move the day.","A DST change moves an hour at most, never a whole day.","A Date is a fixed instant in milliseconds since the epoch. Nothing about it drifts.",""]},{"q":"What does new Date(\"garbage\") do?","options":["Returns an Invalid Date whose every method yields NaN","Returns null, so a truthiness check catches it","Returns the current time as a fallback","Throws a TypeError as soon as the string fails to parse"],"answer":0,"why":"No throw, no null, an Invalid Date object, and every calculation touching it becomes NaN and spreads. Guard with Number.isNaN(d.getTime()) at the boundary.","whyWrong":["","It returns a Date object, and that object is truthy.","There is no fallback to now.","The constructor never throws on an unparseable string."]}],"jsfp":[{"q":"Which property makes a function pure?","options":["It declares every binding inside with const rather than let","It is written as an arrow function rather than a declaration","It stays under ten lines, short enough that the whole body reads at a glance","Same arguments always give the same result, and nothing outside it changes"],"answer":3,"why":"Purity is two promises: deterministic output, and no side effects, no mutated arguments, no shared state, no I/O. That is what makes it testable with a call and an assertion.","whyWrong":["const stops rebinding, and the function can still mutate the world around it.","Syntax has nothing to do with purity.","Length is a style preference. A one-liner can write to a global.",""]},{"q":"items.sort(byDate) versus items.toSorted(byDate), what is the actual difference?","options":["sort reorders the caller's array in place; toSorted returns a sorted copy","toSorted is faster, since it sorts without moving elements around","There is none, toSorted is simply the newer name for the same method","sort only handles numbers unless you hand it a comparator function"],"answer":0,"why":"sort mutates, every other holder of that array sees the new order, a surprise at a distance. toSorted leaves the original untouched, which is the immutability discipline in one method choice.","whyWrong":["","It copies first, so it does slightly more work. Safety is the point.","One mutates and one copies. That is the whole story.","Default sort compares as strings, and a comparator handles any type in both."]},{"q":"const copy = { ...user }, what does this copy, exactly?","options":["Everything at every depth, including nested objects and the arrays inside them","The top level only; nested objects are still shared with the original","Nothing, it creates an alias to the same object","Only the string properties, other types are dropped"],"answer":1,"why":"Spread copies one level. copy.name is independent, but copy.address points at the SAME address object, so the discipline is per-level: copy what you change, at the level you change it.","whyWrong":["Deep copying needs structuredClone; spread stops at one level.","","It is a real new object; only the nested values are shared.","All own enumerable properties come along, whatever the type."]}],"jsgen":[{"q":"What happens when you CALL a generator function, const g = steps()?","options":["The body runs to completion and returns an array","The body runs up to the first yield and pauses","Nothing runs; you get a paused machine that advances on next()","It starts running on a background thread pool"],"answer":2,"why":"Calling a generator executes none of its body. Each next() runs to the following yield and pauses, with all local state preserved between calls.","whyWrong":["It never runs eagerly to completion.","Not even to the first yield, that takes the first next().","","There are no threads here; it is a pausable function."]},{"q":"Why is function* naturals() { let n = 1; while (true) yield n++; } not an infinite loop bug?","options":["Generators are lazy: the body only advances when a consumer asks for the next value","A yield inside while(true) is rejected when the function is parsed","It is a bug, calling naturals() runs the loop straight away and hangs before it returns","The engine caps a generator at 10,000 values and then closes it"],"answer":0,"why":"Nothing runs until next() is called, and each call runs exactly to the next yield. An endless sequence is fine as long as the consumer stops asking.","whyWrong":["","Yielding inside a loop is the normal pattern and parses fine.","Calling it just builds an iterator. No body runs until next() is called.","There is no such cap. A generator yields until you stop asking."]},{"q":"A for...of loop over a generator holding an open resource exits early with break. What happens to the generator's try/finally?","options":["break calls the generator's return(), and the finally runs","The generator keeps running in the background","The finally is skipped (cleanup needs a manual close call)","The finally runs only if the generator was fully consumed"],"answer":0,"why":"Exiting a for...of early, break, throw or return, calls the iterator's return(), which resumes the generator as if a return happened at the paused yield. The finally runs, so cleanup is guaranteed.","whyWrong":["","Nothing runs in the background; the generator finishes right then.","That is why the protocol includes return() (no manual close needed.)","Early abandonment triggers it too, that is the guarantee."]}],"jsemit":[{"q":"bus.emit(\"order\", 42), when do the listeners run?","options":["On the next microtask, like a promise","Synchronously, they have all run before emit returns","On the next timer tick, via setTimeout","In parallel on a pool of worker threads"],"answer":1,"why":"emit calls every listener for the event, in registration order, right now. The line after emit runs after all listeners are done.","whyWrong":["Promises defer; emitters do not.","","Timers are macrotasks; this is a plain function call underneath.","One thread, one loop, listeners are just function calls."]},{"q":"An EventEmitter emits \"error\" and nobody registered an error listener. What does Node do?","options":["Logs a warning and continues","Throws, and an uncaught one crashes the process","Retries the operation that failed","Silently drops it and carries on"],"answer":1,"why":"An error nobody is listening for should not vanish, so Node throws it. Every long-lived emitter, streams, servers, sockets, needs an error listener.","whyWrong":["It is a throw, not a warning.","","Node knows nothing about the operation; it just refuses to swallow the error.","Deliberately not (silent errors are worse than crashes.)"]},{"q":"A request handler does bus.on(\"tick\", () => ...) on a module-level emitter. Why is this the classic leak?","options":["Each request adds a listener that is never removed, so they accumulate","The closure prevents the emitter from being garbage collected","on() is slower than once(), so the handler queue backs up","An emitter refuses new listeners once it is already holding ten of them"],"answer":0,"why":"Every request adds one more listener; none are removed. The emitter references each listener, each closure references what it captured, and none of it can be collected while subscribed. Subscribing is allocating.","whyWrong":["","It is the reverse: the long-lived emitter keeps the listeners alive.","Speed is not the problem here. The listener count is.","Ten is a warning threshold, not a limit, and the warning exists to surface this bug."]}],"jssec":[{"q":"What is the one cure that fixes SQL injection, XSS and command injection alike?","options":["Rejecting any input with quotes, angle brackets or semicolons","Base64-encoding every user value before it reaches the sink","Keeping user data out of the executable channel, as data rather than code","Escaping quotes in user input before building the statement"],"answer":2,"why":"Every injection is user text concatenated into something that gets executed. Parameterised queries, textContent and execFile argument arrays all make the same move: data travels as data, never as code.","whyWrong":["Legitimate input carries those characters constantly, and blocklists always leak.","The decoded value lands in the same channel. Encoding moves nothing.","","Escaping is host-specific and fragile, and it patches the wrong design."]},{"q":"An attacker sends {\"__proto__\": {\"isAdmin\": true}} to an endpoint that deep-merges it into a config object. What can happen?","options":["Object.prototype gains isAdmin, so every object now reports isAdmin as true","Nothing happens: JSON.parse refuses to create a key named __proto__","Only the config object gets the extra property, and nothing outside it is touched","The request is rejected: __proto__ is a reserved key in JSON"],"answer":0,"why":"A naive merge that walks into a __proto__ key writes onto Object.prototype itself, poisoning every object in the program. Refuse the keys __proto__, constructor and prototype in merged input.","whyWrong":["","JSON.parse creates it as an ordinary own property; the risk is what your merge does with it.","A merge that walks into __proto__ writes on the shared prototype, so the damage is global.","It is valid JSON, and nothing rejects it for you."]},{"q":"A pull request's only change to package-lock.json is a surprise you cannot explain. Why treat it seriously?","options":["Lockfile changes always break the build, so a diff touching only the lockfile should be rejected","The lockfile pins the exact code you ship; a changed pin can swap in different code, and installing runs it","Lockfiles are generated artifacts and cannot meaningfully be reviewed by a human reader","It is harmless, since npm regenerates the file from the manifest on the next install anyway"],"answer":1,"why":"The lockfile is a security file: it records the exact bytes npm ci will install, and install scripts execute on your machines. An unexplained pin change deserves the suspicion you would give a binary blob.","whyWrong":["Often it builds fine, that is what makes it easy to wave through.","","It is JSON; diffs are reviewable, which is the point.","npm ci installs exactly what it says, that is why what it says matters."]}],"jsmem":[{"q":"When does the garbage collector free an object?","options":["Immediately when the function that created it returns","When memory pressure forces the OS to intervene","When you set every variable that held it to null","When it becomes unreachable from the roots"],"answer":3,"why":"One rule: alive means findable by following references from a root. Unreachable means garbage, collected whenever the engine pleases.","whyWrong":["Only if nothing escaped the call, a returned closure keeps the scope alive.","Collection is the engine's routine work, not an OS emergency.","Nulling variables is only one way to sever paths, any unreachability counts, and one surviving path keeps it alive.",""]},{"q":"Two objects reference each other in a cycle, and nothing else references either. Are they leaked?","options":["No, reachability is measured from the roots, and no root reaches them; a cycle of garbage is still garbage","Yes, each keeps the other alive, so a reference cycle can never be collected once created","Yes, until the page reloads, at which point the entire heap is discarded along with the cycle","Only if they contain closures, since a closure holds its scope and defeats the collector's analysis"],"answer":0,"why":"Mark-and-sweep starts at the roots. The cycle's internal references are irrelevant if no root leads in, this exact case is why reference counting lost.","whyWrong":["","That is how reference COUNTING would fail; engines do not count.","No reload needed.","Closures change nothing about the rule."]},{"q":"When is a WeakMap the right container?","options":["For attaching data to objects without keeping them alive","Whenever you want faster lookups than Map","For primitive keys like strings and numbers","For storing lists you need to iterate often"],"answer":0,"why":"WeakMap keys are held weakly: when the last real reference to the key object drops, the entry evaporates with it, a cache that cannot leak its keys.","whyWrong":["","Speed is comparable; lifetime is the difference.","Keys must be objects; a primitive cannot be collected out from under you.","WeakMaps are deliberately not iterable, you cannot list keys the collector may be removing."]}],"jswork":[{"q":"What is a worker, in one sentence?","options":["A separate thread with its own event loop, communicating only by message passing","A faster event loop for your main thread, tuned for CPU-heavy callbacks","A promise-based API that offloads work to the GPU when one is available","A hidden background tab that shares the page's variables through the window object"],"answer":0,"why":"Workers add more event loops rather than speeding up yours, separate thread, separate globals, no shared variables, messages as the only channel.","whyWrong":["","Your loop runs at the same speed. The work simply moves to another thread.","No GPU is involved. A worker is an ordinary thread running JavaScript.","Same page, different thread, and no shared window between them."]},{"q":"An endpoint spends 2 seconds waiting on a database query. Should that move to a worker?","options":["Yes, but only with SharedArrayBuffer to pass the rows","Only if there are more than ten concurrent requests","No, awaited I/O does not occupy the loop; a worker only adds copying","Yes, two seconds of waiting would block the event loop for every request"],"answer":2,"why":"While a query is in flight your code is not running, the loop is free for other requests. Workers exist for CPU-bound work, where your code IS the thing occupying the loop.","whyWrong":["Shared memory addresses a different problem entirely.","Concurrency is what the loop already does well for I/O.","","Waiting is not computing; the loop handles thousands of concurrent waits."]},{"q":"You postMessage a class instance to a worker. What arrives?","options":["The instance arrives intact, prototype and methods included","A DataCloneError, because class instances cannot cross the worker boundary","A plain object with the instance's own data properties and no prototype","A live reference, so changes on either side are visible to both"],"answer":2,"why":"Structured clone copies data: own properties survive, the prototype link and methods do not. Functions and DOM nodes refuse to clone at all. Send data, reconstruct behavior on the other side.","whyWrong":["Methods live on the prototype, and the prototype does not travel.","Plain data clones fine; functions and DOM nodes are what throw.","","Messages are deep copies, so neither side sees the other's writes."]}]};
